1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,411
This is ECE 358, video 16 b. The topic is address aggregation, also called supernetting. It covers slides 59 to 63 of chapters 4 and 5. It uses longest prefix matching from video 16 a.

2
00:00:18,861 --> 00:00:37,037
After this video, you can split the block of an I S P into blocks for organizations. You can explain why hierarchical addressing makes routing tables small. And you can aggregate address blocks into summary addresses. The question bank and slide 63 ask this.

3
00:00:37,487 --> 00:00:50,713
Before aggregation, slide 60 shows where a network gets its addresses. It gets a portion of the address space of its provider I S P. The I S P gets its block from ICANN.

4
00:00:51,163 --> 00:01:00,934
Here, the block of the I S P is 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 20. The 20 network bits are purple.

5
00:01:01,384 --> 00:01:05,459
The host part has 32 minus 20, which is 12 bits.

6
00:01:05,909 --> 00:01:12,010
So the block has 2 to the power 12 addresses. That is 4096 addresses.

7
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:20,119
The I S P splits its block into 8 blocks, one for each organization. 8 is 2 to the power 3.

8
00:01:20,569 --> 00:01:25,710
So 3 more bits, bits 21 to 23 in orange, pick the block.

9
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:35,781
Organization 0 gets the bits 0 0 0. That is 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 23.

10
00:01:36,231 --> 00:01:45,149
Organization 1 gets 0 0 1, which is 200 dot 23 dot 18 dot 0, slash 23.

11
00:01:45,599 --> 00:01:54,345
Organization 2 gets 0 1 0, which is 200 dot 23 dot 20 dot 0, slash 23.

12
00:01:54,795 --> 00:02:05,185
The pattern continues to organization 7, with bits 1 1 1. That is 200 dot 23 dot 30 dot 0, slash 23.

13
00:02:05,635 --> 00:02:15,811
The third byte goes up by 2 each time: 16, 18, 20, and so on to 30. Bit 23 has the weight 2 in the third byte.

14
00:02:16,261 --> 00:02:26,863
Each block has 2 to the power 12 addresses, divided by 2 to the power 3. That is 2 to the power 9, or 512 addresses.

15
00:02:27,313 --> 00:02:34,545
So each block has 9 host bits. The prefix is 32 minus 9, which is slash 23.

16
00:02:34,995 --> 00:02:43,678
Slide 61 shows why this allocation helps. Eight organizations get their slash 23 blocks from the same I S P.

17
00:02:44,128 --> 00:02:49,248
They connect to the Internet through router R 1 of Fly-By-Night I S P.

18
00:02:49,698 --> 00:02:54,733
R 1 tells router R 2 in the Internet which addresses it can reach.

19
00:02:55,183 --> 00:03:03,140
Without aggregation, R 1 advertises 8 routes, one slash 23 prefix for each organization.

20
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:16,091
With aggregation, R 1 advertises only 1 route: send me anything with addresses that begin with 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 20.

21
00:03:16,541 --> 00:03:34,440
The single slash 20 prefix covers all 8 slash 23 blocks. Each slash 23 has 512 addresses, and 8 times 512 is 4096, which is 2 to the power 12, the size of the slash 20.

22
00:03:34,890 --> 00:03:39,946
Where is the hierarchy? The slash 20 block of the I S P is the parent.

23
00:03:40,396 --> 00:03:44,833
The 8 slash 23 blocks of the organizations are its children.

24
00:03:45,283 --> 00:03:49,870
Routers outside the I S P, such as R 2, see only the parent.

25
00:03:50,320 --> 00:04:01,520
So hierarchical addressing allows efficient advertisement of routing information. Other routers keep fewer entries, and fewer advertisements cross the network.

26
00:04:01,970 --> 00:04:08,861
Slide 62 shows address aggregation, also called supernetting. Router A has 4 entries.

27
00:04:09,311 --> 00:04:19,017
The entries are 192 dot 168 dot 0, dot 1, dot 2 and dot 3, each slash 24.

28
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:21,793
Write the four addresses in binary.

29
00:04:22,243 --> 00:04:25,357
The first 22 bits are the same in all four.

30
00:04:25,807 --> 00:04:35,023
Only bits 23 and 24 change. They take all four values: 0 0, 0 1, 1 0 and 1 1.

31
00:04:35,473 --> 00:04:48,721
So the four blocks together form one block with a 22 bit prefix: 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0, slash 22. This is the summary address.

32
00:04:49,171 --> 00:04:58,131
Router A advertises: I can reach 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0, slash 22.

33
00:04:58,581 --> 00:05:01,355
Router B keeps 1 entry instead of 4.

34
00:05:01,805 --> 00:05:16,183
Check the size. Each slash 24 has 2 to the power 8, which is 256 addresses. 4 blocks of 256 is 1024, which is 2 to the power 10.

35
00:05:16,633 --> 00:05:25,935
A slash 22 prefix leaves 32 minus 22, which is 10 host bits. That is also 2 to the power 10 addresses.

36
00:05:26,385 --> 00:05:30,502
So the summary covers exactly the four blocks, and nothing else.

37
00:05:30,952 --> 00:05:36,925
Slide 63 does the same for the 8 organization blocks. Write them in binary.

38
00:05:37,375 --> 00:05:41,514
The first 20 bits, in green, are the same in all 8 blocks.

39
00:05:41,964 --> 00:05:49,452
Bits 21 to 23, in orange, take all 8 values, from 0 0 0 to 1 1 1.

40
00:05:49,902 --> 00:05:59,118
So the summary address is 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 20. This is the block of the I S P.

41
00:05:59,568 --> 00:06:13,861
Check the size. Each slash 23 has 2 to the power 9, which is 512 addresses. 8 blocks of 512 is 4096, which is 2 to the power 12.

42
00:06:14,311 --> 00:06:23,634
A slash 20 leaves 32 minus 20, which is 12 host bits. That is 2 to the power 12 addresses. The summary is exact.

43
00:06:24,084 --> 00:06:28,201
The method has three steps. One: write the blocks in binary.

44
00:06:28,651 --> 00:06:30,699
Two: find the common prefix.

45
00:06:31,149 --> 00:06:36,867
Three: check that the summary holds exactly the given blocks, and no other addresses.

46
00:06:37,317 --> 00:06:51,098
If the summary holds extra addresses, split the blocks into groups. Slide 63 says that a possible final exam question is: given a few address blocks, find their summary addresses.

47
00:06:51,548 --> 00:07:02,001
Here is the question on slide 63. Take the 8 networks, but remove network 3. Compute the summary addresses. Pause the video and try it.

48
00:07:02,451 --> 00:07:08,147
Only the third byte changes. Draw it as a number line from 16 to 31.

49
00:07:08,597 --> 00:07:18,581
A slash 23 block leaves 1 host bit in the third byte. So it covers 2 values. Organization 0 covers 16 and 17.

50
00:07:19,031 --> 00:07:29,591
Organization 3 is 200 dot 23 dot 22 dot 0, slash 23. So the values 22 and 23 are missing.

51
00:07:30,041 --> 00:07:34,841
A summary must not cover them. So one slash 20 is not possible.

52
00:07:35,291 --> 00:07:40,603
Start at the left. Organizations 0 and 1 cover 16 to 19.

53
00:07:41,053 --> 00:07:55,539
That is 4 values, and 16 is a multiple of 4. 4 values need 2 host bits in the third byte. So it is one block: 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 22.

54
00:07:55,989 --> 00:08:09,535
Organization 2 covers 20 and 21. It cannot join 22 and 23. So it stays alone: 200 dot 23 dot 20 dot 0, slash 23.

55
00:08:09,985 --> 00:08:28,140
Organizations 4 to 7 cover 24 to 31. That is 8 values, and 24 is a multiple of 8. 8 values need 3 host bits. So it is one block: 200 dot 23 dot 24 dot 0, slash 21.

56
00:08:28,590 --> 00:08:37,721
So the answer is three summary addresses: slash 22, slash 23, and slash 21. Seven entries become three.

57
00:08:38,171 --> 00:08:44,720
The rule: a block of 2 to the power k values must start at a multiple of 2 to the power k.

58
00:08:45,170 --> 00:09:00,359
Question 66: aggregate six destination networks. All six use interface 1. The question bank has no published answers, so this solution uses the method of the slides. Pause the video and try it.

59
00:09:00,809 --> 00:09:06,015
Only the third byte changes. Draw it as a number line from 0 to 7.

60
00:09:06,465 --> 00:09:08,641
The values 1 and 4 are missing.

61
00:09:09,091 --> 00:09:20,867
Value 0 cannot join 1, because 1 is missing. So 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0, slash 24, stays alone.

62
00:09:21,317 --> 00:09:34,095
Values 2 and 3 make a block of 2 that starts at 2, a multiple of 2. So they become 192 dot 168 dot 2 dot 0, slash 23.

63
00:09:34,545 --> 00:09:42,140
Values 5, 6 and 7 are 3 values. 3 is not a power of 2, so they cannot form one block.

64
00:09:42,590 --> 00:09:50,803
Value 5 stays alone: 192 dot 168 dot 5 dot 0, slash 24.

65
00:09:51,253 --> 00:10:02,773
Values 6 and 7 start at 6, a multiple of 2. So they become 192 dot 168 dot 6 dot 0, slash 23.

66
00:10:03,223 --> 00:10:17,901
Why not one entry, 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0 slash 21? That block covers 8 values, 0 to 7. So it also covers the networks 1 and 4.

67
00:10:18,351 --> 00:10:29,529
A packet to 192 dot 168 dot 4 dot 9 would match it and go out on interface 1. But that network is not in the table.

68
00:10:29,979 --> 00:10:38,235
Slide 63 asks for summary addresses, plural, when one net is missing. So a summary must not add addresses.

69
00:10:38,685 --> 00:10:49,800
So the answer is four entries, all on interface 1: slash 24, slash 23, slash 24, slash 23. Six entries become four.

70
00:10:50,250 --> 00:11:02,517
Question 67 has the same six networks. But now networks 5, 6 and 7 use interface 3. Compare it with question 66. Pause the video and try it.

71
00:11:02,967 --> 00:11:06,615
The first group is 0, 2 and 3, on interface 1.

72
00:11:07,065 --> 00:11:19,609
As before, 0 stays alone, and 2 and 3 become 192 dot 168 dot 2 dot 0, slash 23. Both go to interface 1.

73
00:11:20,059 --> 00:11:24,112
The second group is 5, 6 and 7, on interface 3.

74
00:11:24,562 --> 00:11:37,383
As before, 5 stays alone, and 6 and 7 become 192 dot 168 dot 6 dot 0, slash 23. Both go to interface 3.

75
00:11:37,833 --> 00:11:45,556
The difference: an aggregate entry has one interface. So you can only aggregate entries with the same interface.

76
00:11:46,006 --> 00:11:55,819
Here, the missing networks 1 and 4 already split the groups. So the blocks are the same as in question 66. Only the interfaces differ.

77
00:11:56,269 --> 00:12:23,576
The answer: 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0 slash 24 and 192 dot 168 dot 2 dot 0 slash 23 on interface 1. 192 dot 168 dot 5 dot 0 slash 24 and 192 dot 168 dot 6 dot 0 slash 23 on interface 3.

78
00:12:24,026 --> 00:12:39,706
To recap. An I S P block with prefix p, split into 2 to the power k blocks, gives blocks with prefix p plus k. Aggregation advertises one summary route instead of many. Routers keep fewer entries.

79
00:12:40,156 --> 00:12:57,991
To aggregate, write the blocks in binary, find the common prefix, and check that the summary holds exactly the given blocks. A block of 2 to the power k values starts at a multiple of 2 to the power k. Only entries with the same interface can merge.

80
00:12:58,441 --> 00:13:03,475
The next video covers NAT, autonomous systems, and the basics of routing.
